 <div class="seo-hero-cnt">
  <p class="eyebrow">Uintah County, Utah — 13 Miles East of Vernal on US-40</p>
  <h1>Jensen, Utah<br><em>and the Road to the Quarry</em></h1>
  <p>Jensen is the turning for the Utah side of Dinosaur National Monument — and it has no hotels, no groceries and no full services. This page covers what Jensen actually is, what the approach involves, and why visitors base in Vernal.</p>
  <div class="seo-hero-btns">
   <a href="tel:${BUSINESS.phoneTel}" class="btn-p">📞 ${BUSINESS.directBooking.ctaLabel} ${BUSINESS.directBooking.discountPercent}%</a>
   <a href="tel:${BUSINESS.phoneTel}" class="btn-s">📞 ${BUSINESS.phoneDisplay}</a>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<div class="content-wrap">

<div class="intro-pull"><strong>There are no hotels in Jensen, Utah.</strong> That is the single most useful fact about the place, and it is why almost everyone visiting the Utah side of Dinosaur National Monument sleeps in Vernal, about 13 miles west on US-40. Jensen is the turning — the Quarry Visitor Center is 7 miles north of it on Utah State Highway 149.<sup>1</sup> This page is about Jensen and the approach. For the monument itself — fees, the Exhibit Hall shuttle, the Cub Creek drive, camping and how many days to allow — see our <a href="/hotel-near-dinosaur-national-monument" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument guide</a>.</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>1. What Jensen, Utah Actually Is</h2>
<p>Jensen is a small community on US-40 in Uintah County, Utah, roughly 13 miles east of Vernal, where the Green River crosses the highway. Visitors encounter it as a junction rather than a town: it is the point at which Utah State Highway 149 leaves US-40 and runs north toward Dinosaur National Monument.<sup>1</sup></p>
<p>What matters for planning is what Jensen does not have. There is no hotel, no supermarket and no full service centre. A visitor who arrives in Jensen expecting a base has misjudged the geography — the nearest town with lodging, groceries, fuel and restaurants is Vernal, back the way you came.</p>
<p><strong>Disambiguation.</strong> This page is about Jensen in Uintah County, Utah — the community on US-40 at the Utah State Highway 149 turning for Dinosaur National Monument. It is not Jensen Beach in Florida, and it is a separate place from the town of Dinosaur, Colorado, which sits on the far side of the monument in Moffat County, Colorado.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>2. How Far Jensen Is — From Vernal and From the Quarry</h2>
<p>Jensen sits on US-40 roughly 13 miles east of Vernal. The Quarry Visitor Center is a further 7 miles north of Jensen on Utah State Highway 149,<sup>1</sup> making the total from ${BUSINESS.address.full} approximately 20 miles — call it half an hour with the turn. Distances here are approximate; the verified leg is the 7 miles from Jensen to the visitor center.<sup>1</sup></p>
<p>The practical consequence is that Jensen is a waypoint, not a destination. You pass through it twice in a day: once outbound with a full tank and a packed lunch, once inbound on the way back to a room.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>3. The Highway 149 Approach</h2>
<p>Utah State Highway 149 is the road that matters here. It leaves US-40 at Jensen and runs 7 miles north to the Quarry Visitor Center, which is the Utah side of the monument — the side with the dinosaur bones.<sup>1</sup></p>
<p>Two things are worth knowing before you make the turn:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>This is the Utah approach, not the Colorado one.</strong> The monument has two visitor centers in two different states, and only the Utah side has the quarry.<sup>1</sup> If your plan involves the wall of bones, Jensen is the correct turning.</li>
 <li><strong>Entry is by pass, and it is charged at the monument, not at Jensen.</strong> Arrive with a pass or the means to buy one — the amounts are on our <a href="/hotel-near-dinosaur-national-monument" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument guide</a>, along with how the Exhibit Hall itself is reached, which changes by season.<sup>1</sup></li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>4. Things To Do Around Jensen</h2>
<p>Jensen itself is a junction, so the answer is mostly "keep driving" — but two things are close enough to be worth naming:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>The monument, 7 miles north.</strong> The Quarry Exhibit Hall, the Cub Creek drive, the petroglyphs and the Josie Bassett Morris homestead are all covered on the <a href="/hotel-near-dinosaur-national-monument" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument page</a> rather than repeated here.</li>
 <li><strong>The Green River.</strong> The river crosses US-40 at Jensen, and Split Mountain upstream is the main river access on the Utah side.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>5. Seasonal Planning</h2>
<p>Nothing about Jensen changes by season — it is a highway junction. What changes is the approach beyond it:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Summer</strong> — peak season on the monument side. Expect a shuttle rather than driving the last stretch to the Exhibit Hall,<sup>1</sup> which affects where you leave the car after the Highway 149 turn.</li>
 <li><strong>Spring and autumn</strong> — quieter, and the drive up Highway 149 is usually the whole journey.</li>
 <li><strong>Winter</strong> — services on the monument side reduce and road conditions on US-40 and Highway 149 vary. Check current conditions with the Park Service before travelling.</li>
</ul>
<div class="pro-tip"><p><strong>Whatever the season, Jensen will not rescue a plan.</strong> There is no fuel stop you can rely on and nowhere to buy lunch. Sort both in Vernal before you leave — 13 miles west, and the last full services before the monument.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>6. Permits and Regulations</h2>
<p>Nothing is permitted or regulated in Jensen itself. Everything that is applies at the monument, and we link it rather than restate it, because fees, hours and river-permit rules change:</p>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Park hours, fees, shuttle operation and current conditions</strong> — <a href="https://www.nps.gov/dino/index.htm" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument, National Park Service</a></li>
 <li><strong>Camping on the Utah side</strong> — book via <a href="https://www.recreation.gov" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Recreation.gov</a></li>
 <li><strong>Fishing licences</strong> on the Green River — <a href="https://wildlife.utah.gov/guidebooks" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Utah Division of Wildlife Resources</a></li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>7. Planning Your Visit</h2>
<h3>How should you stage the day?</h3>
<p>Treat Vernal as the base and Jensen as the halfway marker. Breakfast and fuel in Vernal, the 13 miles east on US-40, the turn north onto Highway 149, then 7 miles to the visitor center.<sup>1</sup> Reverse it in the evening. How long to allow at the monument itself is covered on the <a href="/hotel-near-dinosaur-national-monument" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">monument guide</a>.</p>
<h3>What has to be done before you reach Jensen?</h3>
<ul class="feat-list">
 <li><strong>Fuel</strong> — fill in Vernal. Do not plan on Jensen.</li>
 <li><strong>Food and water</strong> — nothing is sold at the monument, and Jensen is not a substitute. This is high desert; carry more water than feels necessary.</li>
 <li><strong>Your entry pass</strong> — or the means to buy one at the monument. Nothing is sold in Jensen.</li>
 <li><strong>Directions</strong> — download them. Coverage is unreliable once you leave US-40.</li>
</ul>
<p>All four are Vernal errands. That is the practical argument for basing there rather than pressing east and hoping.</p>
</div>

<div class="content-section">
 <h2>8. Where This Leaves You for Lodging</h2>
<p>Jensen has no answer to the lodging question, so the answer is Vernal — 13 miles back west, and the only town on this stretch of US-40 with hotels, groceries, fuel and restaurants.</p>
<p>Best Western Vernal Inn is at ${BUSINESS.address.full}, which puts the Highway 149 turning about twenty minutes east. What that proximity buys you on a monument day is covered on our <a href="/hotel-near-dinosaur-national-monument" style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:700;">Dinosaur National Monument guide</a>, along with nightly rates from ${LOWEST_NIGHTLY} (${RATES.taxNote}) and weekly rates quoted by the front desk.</p>
</div>
